## 1. Introduction
Porphyrins are macromolecular heterocyclic compounds composed of porphin $(\mathsf{C}_{20}\mathsf{H}_{14}\mathsf{N}_{4})$ substituted by various functional groups at the meso-position or $\beta$ -position.[1] In addition, free-base porphyrins can be coordinated with numerous metal ions in the porphyrin center to form metal complexes, also known as metalloporphyrins.[2] In fact, many porphyrins and metalized porphyrin derivatives exist in nature and organisms and play significant roles, such as cytochrome, heme and chlorophyll. The porphyrin family discovered up to now as functional molecules have been comprehensively studied.[3] Due to their large π- aromatic system, porphyrins possess excellent chemical and thermal stabilities as well as distinct photophysical and electrochemical performances, which can be regulated by the substitution patterns on porphin and the coordinated metal ions. Except for the central metallization, the periphery site of porphyrins can also be bound to metal ions to form peripherally metalized porphyrins.[4] Intricate binding modes enable porphyrins to form desirable molecular cages or framework solids.[5] Porphyrins and porphyrin derivatives are an important class of organic chromophores, with the considerable absorption in the visible section of the electromagnetic spectrum. Normally, the absorption spectrum shows the strongest absorption around $400{\sim}450\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Soret band), and a series of gradually reduced absorption bands between 500 and $700\mathsf{n m}$ (Q-bands).[1]
To date, porphyrins and porphyrin derivatives have been applied for various applications due to their characteristics and versatile functions (Figure 1). For instance, the pronounced visible light absorption and energy migration allow porphyrins to be used in light-harvesting, solar cells and molecular electronics.[6] The chemical catalytic activity of porphyrins, especially metalloporphyrins, render them as efficient photocatalysts, electrocatalysts and biomimetic catalysts.[7] The regulation of optical and electronic properties caused by the coordination of metal ions and the axial coordination of molecules, enable their application in molecular recognition and metal ions sensing.[8] More importantly, porphyrins exist in many biological processes and produce essential effects. Therefore, they possess potent biological performances, such as biocompatibility, effective clearance, longer residence time in tumors, less side effect and mimicking of various biological functions, which are extremely useful for biomedical applications.[9] For example, porphyrins have been widely developed as photosensitizers for photodynamic therapy (PDT). At the same time, their fluorescence characteristics, render porphyrin-based photosensitizers as valuable fluorescence imaging-guided therapy systems.[9b] However, the drawbacks of most of porphyrins and porphyrin derivatives, in particular instability, enzymatic degradation, nonspecific targeting, and propensity to self-quenching in physiological conditions, seriously limit their biomedical applications. To overcome these problems, various carriers were developed to encapsulate or physically adsorb or covalently bind porphyrins and porphyrin derivatives, such as micelles,[10] liposomes,[11] carbon nanotubes,[12] inorganic nanoparticles,[13] and polymer nanoparticles [14].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a category of hybrid porous coordination polymers with two- or three-dimensional (2D/3D) topologies assembled by the coordination of metal ions/secondary building units (SBUs) with organic linkers. As an emerging material, MOFs have recently attracted increasing attention because of their multiple merits including ultra-high porosity,[15] tunable pores, adjustable structure and composition, unsaturated metal sites and functional diversity.[16] Furthermore, the particle size of MOFs can be easily decreased to nanoscale, and nanoscale MOFs (NMOFs) are suitable as delivery systems. Compared with other types of carriers, NMOFs are more promising as versatile nanoplatforms for biomedical applications, such as drug delivery, tumor therapy, bioimaging and biosensing.[17]
Accordingly, it has been conceived that the integration of porphyrins into MOFs could create multifunctional carriers, providing promising characteristics and versatility of the functional components, porphyrins and MOFs. From this perspective, porphyrin-based MOFs are ideal biomaterials and their potential is yet to be exploited. Moreover, the conceptual construcion of porphyrin-based MOFs matches well with advanced design concepts for highly active biomaterials with maximized integrated functional building units while minimizing inactive constituents.[18] Therefore, much effort has been devoted to the development of various porphyrin-based MOF platforms for biomedical applications. Loading of porphyrins into MOFs’ channels or decorating of porphyrins on MOFs’ surface is an efficient strategy to synthesize porphyrin@MOFs, which enhances the stability of porphyrins and facilitates potential applications.[19] Interestingly, free-base porphyrins or metalloporphyrins can aslo be used as organic linkers to assemble porphyrinic or metalloporphyrinic MOFs, resulting in the protection of porphyrins from selfaggregation and self-quenching limitations, and thereby improving the physicochemical properties. Due to the highly active frameworks and improved performance of porphyrins in MOFs, the development of porphyrin-based MOFs is a promising opportunity for biomedical applications.
Numerous excellent reviews have separately addressed porphyrins or MOFs, and their respective potential biomedical applications during the last two decades. However, so far a comprehensive review linking porphyrins to MOFs for biomedical applications is missing. In the following, we summarize the latest achievements and progress in the field of porphyrin-based MOFs, from synthesis strategies all the way to functions and applications. We identify numerous biomedical applications, to provide a concise overview and stimulate the field for this promising direction. Furthermore, we also discuss the challenges and prospects for biomedical applications of porphyrin-based MOFs.
