## 2. Synthesis of porphyrin-based MOFs
An enormous number of MOFs, such as ZIFs (zeolite imidazolate frameworks),[20] MILs (material from Institut Lavoisier) series,[21] and UiOs (University of Oslo materials),[22] have been successfully synthesized, and are designed as efficient platforms to encapsulate or deliver pharmaceuticals, imaging agents or enzymes. For developing biomedical nanoplatforms, the downsizing of MOFs to nanoscale $(10\sim100\mathsf{n m})$ is essential for administration because of the significant influences on the sizedependent functions and biodistribution of administered particles.
To date, a variety of strategies have been proposed to synthesize NMOFs and have been summarized in several reviews.[23] Particularly for the synthesis of NMOFs, several efficient methods have also been developed, including hydrothermal/solvothermal, sonochemical, mechanochemical, microwave-assisted synthesis and reverse microemulsion. However, there are no reports on highlighting the synthesis of porphyrin-based MOFs yet.
Typically, porphyrin-based MOFs can be divided into porphyrin@MOFs and porphyrinic MOFs according to the distinct structures assembled from porphyrins and MOFs. For porphyrin@MOFs, free-base porphyrins or metalloporphyrins as guest molecules are integrated into MOFs via the encapsulation in the pores as well as adsorption or grafting on the surface. While as organic linkers, free-base porphyrins/metalloporphyrins could coordinate with metal ions or SBUs to form porphyrinic/metalloporphyrinic MOFs. On the other hand, porphyrinic/metalloporphyrinic MOFs can be combined with other functional components, like magnetic nanoparticles, photothermal agents and fluorescent quantum dots, to form multifunctional platforms. Actually, the morphology, structure and composition of porphyrin-based MOFs are vital for biomedical applications, and much effort has been devoted to synthesize porphyrin-based MOFs for matching the requirements in biomedical applications.
## 2.1. Synthesis of porphyrin@MOFs
To synthesize porphyrin@MOFs, free-base porphyrins or metalloporphyrins are typically encapsulated into the pores or decorated on the surface of MOFs, and in situ formation and postsynthesis methods are mainly used for the porphyrin-integrating processes (Figure 2).[19] As shown in the scheme, the in situ formation method refers to a one-pot reaction of MOF precursors (i.e., metal nodes and organic linkers) with free-base porphyrins/metalloporphyrins. During the formation of frameworks, free-base porphyrins/metalloporphyrins are simultaneously entrapped inside MOFs like ‘‘ship-in-a-bottle”. In contrast, postsynthesis methods realize the encapsulation into pores or grafting on the surface of MOFs through host-guest reactions between synthetic MOFs and free-base porphyrins/metalloporphyrins, such as hydrogen bonding, van der Waals forces, electrostatic interactions, π-π stacking interactions, covalent bonds or even coordinative bonds.[24] To date, a variety of porphyrin@MOFs have been successfully synthesized, and some typical examples are summarized in Table 1.
Using in situ formation method to encapsulate porphyrins in the interior of MOFs, small pore windows could prevent the leakage of porphyrins as well as facilitate the diffusion of smaller molecules. Eddaoudi et al. for the first time successfully decorated MOFs with porphyrins by $\mathsf{i}n$ situ formation in 2008.[25] The encapsulation of cationic 5,10,15,20-tetrakis(1-methyl-4- pyridinio)porphyrin (TMPyP) in rho-ZMOF (indium-imidazoledicarboxylate-based zeolite-like metal-organic framework) was achieved via one-pot reaction of $\mathsf{I n}^{3+}$ , 4,5-imidazoledicarboxylic acid (H3ImDC) and TMPyP in an $N,N^{\prime}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF)/acetonitrile $(C H_{3}C N)$ solution. Furthermore, Mn, Co, Cu and Zn ions could be used for the metalation of the encapsulated free-base porphyrins.
Due to the simplified synthesis and high efficiency of porphyrin encapsulation by in situ formation method, many groups have attempted to synthesize porphyrin@MOFs as functional platforms for biomedical applications. Among them, the porphyrin@UiO-66 and porphyrin@MIL-88(Fe) have been successfully synthesized, which not only protected the free-base porphyrins or metalloporphyrins by encapuslation, but also consisted of biocompatible Zr and Fe ions.[26] For example, Dong et al. reported the synthesis of UiO-66 for encapsulating the synthetic 5,15-di(4-carboxyphenyl)-10,20-bis(4- iodophenyl)porphyrin zinc(II) $(\mathsf{D}\mathsf{T P P}(\mathsf{Z n})-\mathsf{l}_{2})$ by in situ formation method,[26a] and the loading capacity of DTPP $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathsf{n}})-\boldsymbol{\mathsf{I}}_{2}$ in UiO-66 was estimated to be ca. $1.4~\mathsf{w t\%}$ , which was much higher compared to normal impregnating method $(0.06\mathrm{wt}\%)$ .
For post-synthesis functionalization, the construction of porphyrin@MOFs generally requires appropriate interactions between porphyrins and MOFs, and the following issues require consideration: (i) MOFs should be activated to eliminate solvent molecules in pores or channels before decoration; (ii) the size and shape of porphyrins should match with the MOFs’ pores or channels, which allows the entrance of porphyrins throughout the integration process; (iii) bond formation should be triggered between porphyrin and the framework. For example, Demel et al. developed a phosphinate-based MOF, (ICR-2, Inorganic Chemistry Rez No. 2), which could be decorated with anionic 5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-R-phosphinatophenyl)porphyrins (TPPPi(R), R=methyl, isopropyl, phenyl) by the interaction with the unsaturated metal sites on ICR-2 surface.[27] A higher fraction of TPPPi(R) could be decorated on MOFs compared with the commercially available 5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4- carboxyphenyl)porphyrin (TCPP), which might be attributed to the stronger bond of phosphinic groups in TPPPi(R) to metal ions than carboxylic groups in TCPP.
## 2.2. Synthesis of porphyrinic MOFs
Porphyrins are macromolecular heterocyclic compounds, which can be used as organic linkers to coordinate with metal ions or SBUs to form porphyrinic MOFs. Such porphyrinic MOFs feature porphyrin functionality but also provide a high porosity to further host secondary functional components. Robson et al. for the first time reported porphyrinic MOFs in 1991,[28] which were assembled by 5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-pyridyl)porphyrin palladium(II) $(\mathsf{T P}\mathsf{y P}(\mathsf{P d}))$ as linkers and $\mathsf{C d}^{2+}$ ions as nodes. Interestingly, SBUs, also termed metal clusters, could be formed through stable metal-oxygen bonds or metal-nitrogen bonds. Such units play a key role for improving structural stability, generating more coordination sites, promoting frameworks extension, avoiding structural interpenetration and expanding pores in MOFs. Therefore, SBUs as nodes can construct robust porphyrinic MOFs with high stability, and recently increasing attention has emerged due to their potential biomedical applications.[29] In 2002, Suslick et al. for the first time reported a stable porphyrinic MOF, (PIZA-1, porphyrinic Illinois zeolite analogue No. 1) by using SBUs as nodes,[30] which was synthesized via self-assembly of trinuclear Co(II)-carboxylate clusters and Co(III)-metalloporphyrin linkers through a solvothermal treatment. This porphyrinic MOFs exposed large and refillable tri-directional channels, and exhibited significant hydrophilic character.
The rational choice of porphyrins plays a vital role in regulating the pores, shapes and sizes of porphyrinic MOFs during synthesis process.[31] Among them, carboxy-based porphyrins have been widely used as linkers to synthesize porphyrinic MOFs, such as TCPP and its metalized molecules (TCPP(M)).[31a] Generally, free-base porphyrins can form porphyrinic MOFs without metal chelating, while the cores of freebase porphyrins can be pre-metalized or metalized by in situ chelation or post chelation with various metal ions to generate metalloporphyrinic MOFs. On the other hand, it is interesting that weakly coordinated metal ions can be exchanged by other affine metal ions,[32] and thereby achieve alternative approaches to synthesize stable porphyrinic MOFs.
To date, various metal nodes, such as Cu, Zn, Co and Cd ions, have been used to assemble with TCPP or TCPP(M) linkers to form 2D MOFs (Figure 3).[6e, 33] For example, Zhang et al. reported for the first time a surfactant-assisted method to synthesize homogeneous ultrathin 2D MOF nanosheets (ZnTCPP) with a thickness of sub-10 nm.[33c] In 2D Zn-TCPP nanosheets, one TCPP ligand was linked by four Zn paddlewheel metal nodes $(Z{\mathsf{n}}_{2}({\mathsf{C O O}})_{4})$ and the TCPP ligand was metallized by Zn ions in the course of the formation. This strategy was also used to prepare several different types of ultrathin 2D porphyrinic MOF nanosheets, including Cu-TCPP, Cd-TCPP and Co-TCPP. Recently, Hong et al. utilized TCPP(Fe) ligands to link various divalent metal ions (Zn, Co and Cu) for constructing a series of 2D metalloporphyrinic MOF nanosheets.[33e]
However, the coordination of TCPP or TCPP(M) with Mn, Fe, Zr, Hf-based clusters relatively easy forms porphyrinic MOFs with typical 3D topologies (Figure 3).[34] Zhou et al. reported several porphyrinic MOFs (porous coordination networks, PCNs) with different topologies by a solvothermal method,[35] in which porphyrinic MOFs such as PCN-222, PCN-223, PCN-224 and PCN-225 were built from TCPP or TCPP(M) $\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{N i}$ , Cu, Zn, Co, Mn or Fe) and Zr6 clusters. Importantly, the $Z r_{6}$ clusters with high connectivity and high charge density afford very strong $Z r-O$ bonds, which contributes to high stability of PCNs in acidic or alkaline aqueous solutions. By analogy with PCNs, Yaghi et al. synthesized MOF-525 and MOF-545 by coordinating TCPP with
$Z r_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}$ and $Z r_{6}O_{8}(H_{2}O)_{8}$ units, respectively.[34d] Further, MOF525 and MOF-545 could be post-metalized with $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ to yield metalloporphyrinic MOFs without losing their high surface area and chemical stability.
Interestingly, many studies have proposed the coordination between metal nodes and two or more kinds of linkers to construct multivariate MOFs for introducing multiple functionalities into MOFs.[36] Here, the introduction of other organic linkers into porphyrinic MOFs can regulate the physicochemical properties of porphyrins or endow frameworks with additional functions. For instance, Zhou et al. developed a photochromic MOF (SO-PCN, singlet oxygen-generating porous coordination network), which was constructed by self-assembly of dual linkers, 1,2-bis(2- methyl-5-(pyridin-4-yl)thiophen-3-yl)cyclopent-1-ene (BPDTE) and TCPP, with Zn nodes.[36a] Due to the integration of a photochromic switch BPDTE into porphyrinic MOFs, SO-PCN showed a reversible control over photosensitization of porphyrins by an energy transfer process upon irradiation at specific wavelengths, and thereby met the requirements for photo-control applications.
Nowadays, various porphyrinic MOFs with different structures have been synthesized due to the diversity of porphyrins and metal nodes. Similar to other MOFs, the crystal down-sizing of porphyrinic MOFs to the nanoscale can also be achieved to improve the physiochemical and biological properties for biomedical applications. Solvothermal synthesis is by far the most common method for synthesizing porphyrinic NMOFs.[35d, 37] Here, the types of metal nodes and porphyrins, the reaction conditions, solvent species, stoichiometry, molecular modulators, temperature and reaction time, are the key factors to control the size and morphology of porphyrinic NMOFs, which could be carefully considered in synthesis process. Table 2 summarizes typical synthesis schemes for porphyrinic MOFs in biomedical applications.
## 2.3. Synthesis of composite porphyrinic MOFs
To endow porphyrinic MOFs with multifunctionality, the incorporation of functional components into porphyrinic MOFs to construct composite porphyrinic MOFs is a useful strategy. To date, various functional components (e.g., magnetic nanoparticles, upconversion nanoparticles (UCNPs), biomolecules) have been introduced to form composite porphyrinic MOFs for biomedical applications.[38] For example,
Zhang et al. reported the growth of porphyrinic MOFs on the surfaces of polydopamine nanoparticles, graphene oxide sheets and gold nanorods to form core-shell composite porphyrinic MOFs.[38g] This strategy was driven by controlling the coordination interactions between functional groups of nanostructures and Zr metal nodes, which could not only avoid the self-nucleation of MOFs in solution, but also control the thickness of porphyrinic MOFs. More interestingly, Li et al. reported in situ growth of porphyrinic MOFs on the polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP)-coated lanthanide-doped UCNPs to form UCNPs/PMOF heterodimers with asymmetric compositions.[38c]
From this point of view, the surface interactions including electrostatic forces, hydrophobic interactions, covalent or coordinate bonds, are critical factors to control composite porphyrinic MOFs with well-defined morphology. Therefore, surface modifications are necessary for the fabrication of composite porphyrinic MOFs. The intricate interactions in composite porphyrinic MOFs also affect the degradation characteristics. For example, the high affinity of Zr cations to phosphate anions can result in the disassembly of Zr-based porphyrinic MOFs. Ren et al. evaluated the degradation degree of platinum-decorated PCN-224 (PCN-224-Pt) and PCN-224 in phosphate-buffered saline (PBS).[39] The results indicated that PCN-224-Pt degraded and released TCPP ligands more slowly than PCN-224 due to the strong covalent interaction between Pt and PCN-224. Moreover, surface decorating MOFs with biocompatible composition (e.g., polyethylene glycol (PEG), PVP)
can improve their stability and prolong circulation time in biological environments. Despite a variety of composite porphyrinic MOFs have been synthesized, the complicated synthesis process, low yield and high wasteful production still restrict their developments in biomedical applications. Table 3 summarizes some typical examples of composite porphyrinic MOFs for biomedical applications.
## 3. Biomedical applications of porphyrin-based MOFs
Porphyrin-based MOFs offer a variety of attractive features making use of the integration of porphyrins into MOFs, in particular excellent photophysical and electrochemical properties, porous structure, modular functionalization and biocompatibility. These characteristics are beneficial for the applications of porphyrin-based MOFs in biomedicine, which have attracted more and more attention. Nowadays, numerous interesting biomedical applications, such as drug delivery, tumor therapy, bioimaging and biosensing, have been developed for porphyrinbased MOFs. In the following, we summarize recent progress in the area of biomedical applications of porphyrin-based MOFs, including photodynamic therapy (PDT), synergistic therapy, imaging-guided therapy and biosensing (Figure 4).
## 3.1. Photodynamic therapy of porphyrin-based MOFs
PDT is an efficient tumor therapy strategy that requires three essential factors: photosensitizer, light and molecular oxygen in cells.[40] As a result, PDT generates highly cytotoxic reactive oxygen species (ROS) under light irradiation. ROS including singlet oxygen $({}^{1}\mathsf{O}_{2})$ , superoxide anion radical $(\mathsf{O}_{2}^{-\bullet})$ and hydroxyl radical (•OH) can induce tumor cell death through apoptosis and/or necrosis, and even tumor immunity. By intravenous injection of photosensitizers for accumulation in tumor tissue and local light exposure to tumor sites, PDT can selectively destroy tumor cells while damages to the surrounding normal cells and tissues are minimized.[41] This approach profits from being noninvasive and reduced side effects (e.g., radiation damage, drug toxicity) compared to conventional surgery, radiotherapy and chemotherapy.
Porphyrin and its derivatives are excellent photosensitizers for PDT and several of them have been approved for clinical trials. For porphyrin-based MOFs, the high porphyrin loading capacity and free diffusion of oxygen and ROS allow the highly efficient PDT due to their porous structures. Furthermore, the biocompatible and biodegradable characteristics of MOFs result in enhanced biosafety during PDT. Since the first report on porphyrinic NMOFs for potential tumor PDT by Lin et al. in 2014,[42] numerous porphyrin-based MOFs have been designed for PDT until now, and prototypical progress reports are summarized in Table 1, Table 2 and Table 3.
## 3.1.1. Enhanced photodynamic therapy
In general, the quantity of $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ generated by photosensitizers upon light irradiation is the key factor for PDT effects, which can be regulated by the efficiency of intersystem crossing (ISC) from singlet state (S1) to triplet state $(T_{1})$ of photosensitizers.[43] Interestingly, the introduction of heavy atoms into photosensitizers is well known to induce an enhanced effect on ISC, termed as “heavy atom effect”. In porphyrin-based MOFs, the interactions between porphyrins and metal ions can improve the photodynamic properties of porphyrins on account of “heavy atom effect”, and thereby increase the production of $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ [40a]
The first report on porphyrinic MOFs for PDT was the use of DBP-UiO nanoplatelets, which was assembled by 5,15-di(pbenzoato)porphyrin (DBP) as linkers and $\mathsf{H}\mathsf{f}^{4+}$ as metal nodes (Figure 5).[42] DBP-UiO nanoplates had a high porphyrin loading capacity of 77 wt $\%$ , and showed at least twice as efficient $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ production as free DBP. Correspondingly, DBP-UiO nanoplates presented highly enhanced PDT efficacy against human head and neck tumor cells (SQ20B) compared to free DBP. Therefore, the coordination of porphyrin linkers with heavy metal nodes in porphyrinic MOFs exhibited an enhancement on PDT, caused by the “heavy atom effect”, and the same phenomenon was observed in other porphyrinic MOFs.[33d, 39, 44] For example, GdTCPP MOF nanosheets, which were linked by TCPP with Gd ions, showed an improved photosensitive activity compared to free TCPP.[33d] On the other hand, the encapsulation of porphyrins in MOFs can also enhance ISC together with the $^10_{2}$ generation. Lei et al. encapsulated TMPyP into HKUST-1, and found that the narrowed $\mathsf{S}_{1}\ –\mathsf{T}_{1}$ energy gap of TMPyP caused by the interaction between the encapsulated TMPyP and $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ nodes enhanced the ISC from $\mathsf{S}_{1}$ to $T_{1}$ . As a result, TMPyP $@$ HKUST-1 exhibited higher $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ production capability compared to free TMPyP, and thereby induced higher phototoxicity to tumor cells.[45]
To optimize the photophysical properties and PDT efficacy of porphyrin-based MOFs, the reduction of porphyrins to chlorins by the hydrogenation of the cross-conjugated double bond of porphyrin ring is also an effective strategy, which could result in a red-shift of absorption and an increase of the extinction coefficient.[46] Lin et al. showed the first chlorin-based NMOFs (DBC-UiO) which were synthesized by ${\mathsf{H}}{\mathsf{f}}^{4+}$ nodes and 5,15-di(pbenzoato)chlorin (DBC), and found an enhanced PDT effect for colon tumors.[47] There was a $13\mathsf{n m}$ red shift in absorption and an 11-fold augment in the extinction coefficient of longestwavelength Q band. Consequently, DBC-UiO NMOFs exhibited three times as efficient as DBP-UiO NMOFs in the generation of singlet oxygen as well as much stronger PDT effect in dual colon tumor cell lines (CT26 and HT29). Therefore, using chlorins to construct NMOFs provides nanoplatforms for realizing highly efficient PDT owing to the improved photophysical properties of the reduced porphyrins.
Generally, the accumulation of photosensitizers in tumor tissues and further uptake by tumor cells facilitates an efficient PDT. It is well known that the size of NMOFs is of crucial importance during cellular uptake.[48] To date, many studies have reported to optimize the particle sizes of MOFs and further enhance PDT efficacy. For example, Zhou et al. synthesized PCN-224 with a particle size from 30 to $190\mathsf{n m}$ to investigate the effect of particle sizes on cellular uptake and PDT efficacy.[35d] These PCN-224 nanoparticles showed different cellular uptake by Hela cells, and the 90 nm-sized PCN-224 had a maximal cellular uptake amount determined by inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS). Furthermore, the PDT for Hela cells indicated that the 90 nm-sized PCN-224 induced $81\%$ cell apoptosis after PDT treatment, which was much higher than that induced by other sized PCN-224, suggesting that the sizedependent cellular uptake of NMOFs determines PDT efficacy.
## 3.1.2. Light-controlled photodynamic therapy
Controllable generation of $^10_{2}$ is useful for enhancing PDT efficacy, which requires the release of cytotoxic $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ only in tumor sites and less damage to normal cells and tissues.[49] Light, as an essential exogenous condition for PDT, has been utilized to control the generation of $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ from porphyrin-based MOFs in PDT, and thereby enhance PDT efficacy and reduce toxic side effects. For example, Zhou et al. synthesized photochromic SO-PCN MOFs by the integration of  photochromic switch molecule BPDTE (1,2-bis(2-methyl-5-(pyridin-4-yl)thiophen-3-yl)cyclopent-1-ene) into porphyrinic MOFs, and the SO-PCN MOFs showed a reversible control of $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ production via a competition of energy transfer pathway upon specific wavelength irradiation.[36a] Here, the open and closed state of BPDTE can be reversibly transformed upon photoirradiation, resulting in different photophysical properties (i.e., diverse energy levels). In the SOPCN MOFs, BPDTE upon irradiation at $\lambda=365$ nm induced the open state, and the energy transfer occurred from TCPP excited triplet state to $^3{\sf O}_{2}$ , stimulating the generation of $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ . However, the irradiation $\langle\lambda>450\ \mathsf{n m}\rangle$ ) triggered BPDTE transformation to the closed state, whose energy level was lower than that of TCPP triplet state, and thereby the energy transfer happened to the closed BPDTE, resulting in the quenching of $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ . Therefore, the introduction of BPDTE in porphyrinic MOFs could modulate $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ regeneration and show great potential for controllable PDT.
However, the absorption spectra of porphyrins are mostly in visible light range, resulting in poor tissue penetration, and thereby limits for in vivo PDT with porphyrin-based MOFs. Nearinfrared (NIR) light has advantages over visible light including deeper tissue penetration, higher sensitivity and resolution, which makes it attractive in harvesting NIR light in porphyrin-based MOFs for NIR-triggered PDT. Some studies demonstrated that lanthanide-doped UCNPs can transform NIR light to visible light, and thereby excite the photosensitizers under NIR irradiation for enhanced PDT.[50]
Recently, several studies reported the combination of porphyrin-based MOFs with UCNPs for NIR-triggered PDT to enhance therapeutic efficacy.[38c, 38d] Li et al. reported the heterodimers (UCMOFs) that were composed of PCN-224 and PVP-coated lanthanide-doped UCNPs for NIR-triggered tumor therapy.[38c] UCNPs absorbed NIR light and emitted visible light for energy transferring to TCPP in PCN-224, resulting in the $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ generation. The results revealed that the cytotoxic $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ could be generated from UCMOFs instead of PCN-224 upon $980\mathsf{n m}$ laser irradiation. Moreover, in vivo antitumor activity tests confirmed the significant NIR-triggered PDT ability of UCMOFs. Such strategy enables porphyrin-based MOFs with NIR light harvesting functionality for NIR-triggered PDT of large or deep tumors. He et al. also developed MOF-UCNP core-shell nanoparticles by DNAmediated assembly of PCN-224 with UCNPs.[38d] Upon NIR laser irradiation, MOF-UCNP nanoparticles could produce $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ and the yield increased with the loading of UCNPs. The results showed that less cell death was observed after $980\mathsf{n m}$ laser irradiation for PCN-224 without UCNPs, but a reduction in cell viability of $63.7\%$ occurred after NIR irradiation for MOF-UCNP nanoparticles, which indicated a great potential of MOF-UCNP nanoparticles for NIR-triggered PDT.
## 3.1.3. Positive targeted photodynamic therapy
In general, nanoparticles have a passive targeting ability for improving accumulation at tumor site due to enhanced permeability and retention (EPR) effect. However, surface modification can configure nanoparticles with an active targeting ability, facilitating precise positioning of targeted site and promoting cellular uptake. Due to abundant functional groups and metal nodes on the surface, porphyrin-based MOFs can be easily modified by host-guest reactions, and now various porphyrinbased MOFs have been modified with targeting moieties for enhanced PDT.[35d, 36e, 51]
Zhou et al. modified PCN-224 NMOFs with folic acid (FA), of which the receptor is overexpressed in tumor cells,[35d] through the coordination between carboxylate groups of FA and $Z\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}}}_{6}$ clusters. The results showed that FA-modified PCN-224 NMOFs had better cellular uptake by FAR-positive Hela cells than PCN-224 NMOFs due to the FA receptor-mediated endocytosis, and further improved PDT efficacy against to Hela cells. Zhang et al. reported hyaluronic acid (HA)-coated Zr(IV)-based porphyrinic NMOFs (PZM) for enhanced PDT due to the CD44-targeting of HA to CD44-overexpressed tumor cells.[51] The in vivo results confirmed that more HA-coated PZM NMOFs were distributed in CD44- positive tumor sites compared to PZM NMOFs, and exhibited remarkable tumor growth inhibition.
In addition, modification of porphyrin-based MOFs with specific biomolecules, such as cell membranes, DNA and antibody, also provides an active targeting ability.[38d, 52] For instance, Zhang’s group and Cheng’s group decorated the porphyrin-based NMOFs with tumor cell membranes for immune escape and homologous targeting.[52b, 52c] Tumor cells possess the features of immune tolerance and tumor homologous binding, which are closely associated with their specific plasma membrane proteins. Interestingly, Zhao et al. established a universal method for DNA functionalization of MOFs via surface coordination chemistry, and further modified PCN-224 NMOFs with DNA aptamers (AS1411), which endowed PCN-224 NMOFs with specific molecular recognition capability for specific targeting of human breast cancer cells MDA-MB-231.[52a]
## 3.1.4. Tumor microenvironment-associated photodynamic therapy
Porphyrins show great promising for PDT, but the oxygen dependence limits the therapeutic efficacy of PDT owing to the hypoxia phenomenon in most tumor cells/tissues.[53] Moreover, the oxygen consumption in PDT is faster than the oxygen supply by tumor blood vessels, which further aggravates hypoxia in tumor cells/tissues, and thereby decreases PDT efficacy.[54] To overcome hypoxia in tumor microenvironment, combining MOFs with other adjuvants (e.g., oxygen carriers, peroxidase and interference agents of oxygen consumption [51, 55]) or using its own ingredients (e.g., Cu, Fe, Mn metal ions in MOF skeletons for Fenton-like reactions [56]), is an efficient strategy to increase the intratumoral $\O_{2}$ level, and thereby achieve better therapeutic efficacy for PDT.
Ren et al. reported platinum-decorated PCN-224 (PCN-224- Pt) NMOFs for enhanced PDT (Figure 6).[39] Due to higher level of $H_{2}O_{2}$ in tumor microenvironment, Pt nanoparticles with catalase-like activity could catalyze the decomposition of intratumoral ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ to generate $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ , which could facilitate the further generation of cytotoxic $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ to kill tumor cells. In vitro results showed that PCN-224-Pt presented much higher lethality upon $638\ \mathsf{n m}$ laser irradiation than that of PCN-224 in the hypoxia conditions. In vivo antitumor potential of PCN-224-Pt in H22 tumor-bearing mice model confirmed that tumor growth was completely inhibited after the injection of PCN-224-Pt upon irradiation, but only partial tumor inhibition was observed for PCN224 after PDT. Lin et al. proposed to use 5,10,15,20-tetra $_{p-}$ benzoato)porphyrins (TBP) as linkers and $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}$ clusters as metal nodes for the synthesis of porphyrinic NMOFs (Fe-TBP), in which the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}$ clusters could decompose the intratumoral ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ through Fenton reaction to produce more $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ for PDT and thereby effectively conquer cellular hypoxia.[56a] Compared to free TBP and Hf-TBP NMOFs constructed from Hf-based clusters and TBP linkers, Fe-TBP NMOFs showed highest PDT efficacy under both normoxic and hypoxic conditions. Obviously, Fe-TBP NMOFs are promising for enhanced PDT in hypoxia due to the use of inherent ingredients in porphyrinic MOFs without the addition of other adjuvant agents.
In addition, the increase of ROS level can be achieved by decreasing the glutathione (GSH) level in tumor cells, because GSH can weaken the ROS from the photosensitizers and further decrease PDT efficacy.[57] To decrease GSH level in tumor cells, Li et al. constructed metalloporphyrinic NMOFs (MOF-2) that assembled from ${\mathsf{A l}}^{3+}$ nodes and TCPP(Cu) linkers.[58] As active center of MOF-2, $\mathsf{C u^{2+}}$ could specifically bind and absorb GSH followed by the decrease of GSH level, and thereby increase the ROS level. Compared to NMOFs without $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ center (MOF-1), MOF-2 has GSH-binding role and generated higher concentration of ROS. In vitro results showed that MOF-2 exhibited comparable therapeutic effect with antineoplastic drug camptothecin (CPT). It offers an interesting idea for controlling tumor microenvironment by directly decreasing intracellular GSH level to enhance PDT efficacy.
Specific tumor microenvironment-associated controllable $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ release is also an attractive strategy for enhanced PDT.[59] For instance, Tang et al. developed a bimetallic porphyrinic MOF (NP1), which could be activated to control $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ release for PDT by hydrogen sulfide $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S})$ signaling molecules in a colon adenocarcinoma tumor microenvironment.[59a] NP-1 was synthesized by the self-assembly of TCPP(Zn) linkers and $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ nodes. In vitro results showed that NP-1 NMOFs were activated by ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}$ in cells to induce apoptosis of tumor cells upon irradiation due to the $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ release from NP-1 NMOFs triggered by H2S in cells. In vivo antitumor results also demonstrated the high ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}$ levels in colon adenocarcinoma tumors significantly enhanced the antitumor efficacy owing to the ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}$ -responsive PDT.
More recently, Jiang et al. found that 2D Cu-TCPP MOF nanosheets exhibited selective generation of $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ in tumor microenvironment and depletion of GSH, presenting the augmented tumor therapeutic efficacy.[60] Here, the TCPP linkers in nanosheets possess peroxidase-like activity in response to ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ and acidic pH in tumor microenvironment, and further reduced to peroxyl radicals (ROO·) with the help of peroxidaselike Cu-TCPP MOF nanosheets and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ ions. The $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ could be generated in the spontaneous recombination reaction of ROO· according to the Russell mechanism. Furthermore, GSH could be depleted and converted into oxidized glutathione (GSSG) by the cycle conversion of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{1+}$ in Cu-TCPP MOF nanosheets. Consequently, Cu-TCPP MOF nanosheets selectively kill tumor cells without side effects both in vitro and in vivo. In this view, these therapeutic strategies based on tumor microenvironment are capable of avoiding oxygen dependence and light penetration limitation in PDT, offering some inspirations on tumor treatments.
## 3.2. Synergistic therapy of porphyrin-based MOFs
To date, numerous therapy modalities based on nanoplatforms, including chemotherapy, PDT, photothermal therapy (PTT), radiotherapy (RT), immunotherapy and gas therapy, have been developed as effective techniques for treating malignant tumors.[59c, 61] However, single therapy modality often fails to achieve ideal therapeutic efficacy due to suffering from some limitations, such as multidrug resistance, oxygen dependence, nonspecific heating, and serious side effects. Hence, much effort has been devoted to developing multifunctional nanoplatforms, which integrate two or more therapy modalities into a single system. Porphyrin-based MOFs exhibit excellent PDT efficacy due to the photophysical property of porphyrins, and porous structures endow them with controlled drug delivery. Furthermore, porphyrin-based MOFs can integrate other functional components to obtain multifunctionality. Hence porphyrin-based MOFs are able to combine with two or more efficient therapy modalities for tumor therapy, that can achieve synergistic effects for maximizing therapeutic efficacy.
## 3.2.1. Synergistic chemo- and photodynamic therapy
Combination of PDT and chemotherapy is an efficient dualmodality therapy that have shown enhanced therapeutic efficacy and negligible toxicity.[62] Here, PDT can generate toxic ROS in tumor cells/tissues that induces oxidative damage to intracellular protein and DNA, resulting in high sensitivity of tumor cells to toxic chemotherapeutic drugs.[56b, 63] Thus, the use of lower doses of chemotherapeutic drugs could bring desired antitumor effects with reduced toxic effect on normal cells/tissues. The open porosity, stable structure and low cytotoxicity of porphyrin-based MOFs allow them as drug delivery carriers to encapsulate chemotherapeutics, and the intrinsic nature of porphyrin-based MOFs supports satisfactory drug-release behavior to enhance therapeutic efficacy.[64] Hence, drug-loaded porphyrin-based MOFs show great potentials for synergistic chemo- and photodynamic therapy.
Yin et al. reported biocompatible zirconium porphyrinic NMOFs (NPMOFs) for synergistic chemotherapy and PDT.[37a] NPMOFs showed a doxorubicin (DOX) loading efficiency as high as $109\%$ (w/w), which was attributed to the noncovalent interactions between DOX and NPMOFs, such as π–π stacking effects, hydrophobic interactions and electrostatic interactions. Drug release profiles showed a very slow DOX release rate in normal biological environment (only $10.3\%$ of DOX release at pH 7.0 after 72 h), but a fast DOX release behavior in tumor microenvironment $(58.5\%$ of DOX release at $\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}5.0$ after 72 h). Such drug release behavior is beneficial for a high therapeutic efficacy against tumor cells and low side effect to normal cells. On the other hand, $59.8\%$ of porphyrin in NPMOFs resulted in excellent PDT efficacy. $90\%$ of tumor cell apoptosis was observed by synergistic therapy of DOX@NPMOFs upon 655 nm laser irradiation. In vivo therapy for HepG2 tumor-bearing mice achieved an enhanced therapeutic efficacy for DOX@NPMOFs compared to single chemotherapy or PDT, indicating biocompatible porphyrin-based MOFs are highly promising for synergistic chemotherapy and PDT.
For light-controlled release of toxic chemotherapeutic drugs and enhanced PDT, Zhang et al. developed core-shell composite nanoparticles (AuNR@MOFs) by the growth of porphyrinic MOFs on gold nanorods (AuNR) (Figure 7),[38a] which was used for synergistic chemotherapy and phototherapy due to the welldefined pore structure as drug carrier, TCPP as photosensitizer and AuNR as photothermal agent. Using camptothecin (CPT) as a model drug, AuNR@MOFs achieved controllable CPT release by $808\ \mathsf{n m}$ laser irradiation. On the other hand, AuNR@MOFs exhibited an enhanced ability to produce $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ compared to single porphyrinic NMOFs upon $660~\mathsf{n m}$ laser irradiation due to the
enhanced light absorption and strong electromagnetic field effect of AuNR surface. Consequently, AuNR@MOFs with NIRtriggered drug release and phototherapy showed significant synergistic efficacy for killing tumor cells in vitro and inhibiting tumor growth and metastasis in vivo.
## 3.2.2. Synergistic photothermal and photodynamic therapy
Similar with PDT, PTT is also a targeted and local therapeutic way with minimal invasiveness and high efficacy, which uses the photothermal agents to generate heat upon NIR irradiation to ablate tumor cells and tissues.[41] Differently, PTT is an oxygenindependent and ROS-free process mediated by photothermal agents upon NIR irradiation. Hence, the combination of PDT and PTT for tumor treatment could synergistically enhance the therapeutic effect, and reduce side effects.[65] On one hand, appropriate heat generated by PTT can improve blood flow as well as oxygen supply, and thereby intensify sensitivity of tumor cells to oxygen-dependent PDT; on the other hand, the ROS produced by PDT can interfere with tumor physiology and change its microenvironment, and thereby increases heat sensitivity of tumor cells.[66]
Recently, several studies on porphyrin-based MOFs for synergistic PDT and PTT have been reported. Zhang et al. proposed to assemble porphyrinic MOFs on polydopamine (PDA) nanoparticles to form core-shell nanoparticles (PDA@MOF),[38g] and the photodynamic activity of porphyrinic MOFs and photothermal effect of PDA nanoparticles could contribute PDT and PTT for tumors. The results indicated that PDA@MOF nanoparticles efficiently generated intracellular ROS upon irradiation with $630\ \mathsf{n m}$ laser, and had excellent photothermal conversion upon irradiation with $808~\mathsf{n m}$ laser. Only $18\%$ of cells viability was observed after the incubation with PDA@MOF nanoparticles and irradiation by $630\mathsf{n m}$ and $808{\mathsf{n m}}$ lasers, which was much lower than that by single laser irradiation, suggesting the synergistic tumor inhibition performance.
Besides composite porphyrinic NMOFs, porphyrinic NMOFs without the integration of other PTT agents also have been developed for synergistic PTT and PDT, which shorten the synthesis procedures and improve biosafety. For example, Yang et al. reported novel multifunctional metalloporphyrinic NMOF (ZrFeP) shuttles for PDT and low-temperature PTT synergistic treatment.[66] Zr-FeP NMOFs were assembled from $Z\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}}}_{6}$ clusters and TBP(Fe) metalloporphyrins and loaded with an inhibitor of heat shock protein 70 (Hsp70). The results demonstrated that ZrFeP NMOFs enabled concurrently to catalyze endogenous ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ for the generation of abundant ·OH due to Fenton effect of ion centers, and produced toxic $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ upon laser irradiation. Furthermore, Zr-FeP NMOFs showed obvious photothermal effect and the photothermal conversion efficiency was up to $33.7\%$ . Consequently, upon a 635nm laser irradiation, the Hsp70- loaded Zr-FeP NMOFs efficiently suppressed the tumor growth by synergistic effect of PDT and low-temperature PTT.
Recently, Li et al. proposed to use Cu-TCPP MOF nanosheets as phototherapy nanosystem for synergistic PTT and PDT.[67] The d-d energy band transition of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ and the ultrathin characteristic enabled Cu-TCPP MOF nanosheets to have strong NIR absorption and excellent photothermal performance. The 808 nm photothermal conversion efficiency of Cu-TCPP MOF nanosheets was estimated to be $36.8\%$ and $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ could be generated upon $660~\mathsf{n m}$ irradiation, which induced efficient synergistic PTT and PDT in vitro and in vivo.
## 3.2.3. Synergistic immuno- and photodynamic therapy
Immunotherapy has got important status in tumor therapy owing to the abilities to stimulate individual’s own immune system to recognize and kill primary tumor cells as well as inhibit metastatic tumor cells.[68] Checkpoint blockade immunotherapy, which adopts antibodies or small molecules to block negative immune regulatory pathways for priming antitumor immune, has been successfully used in clinic. Studies have demonstrated that tumor cells themselves can induce T cell apoptosis via programmed cell death receptor 1 (PD-1)/programmed death 1 ligand (PD-L1) pathway for immune evasion. Consequently, PD1/PD-L1 checkpoint blockade strategy is proposed to enhance antitumor immunity by inhibiting cytotoxic T lymphocytes exhaustion. However, such immunotherapy elicits confined rates of systemic antitumor response for many types of tumors according to insufficient activation of the host immune systems.[69] Interestingly, PDT can excite adaptive immune responses through releasing inflammatory mediators and cytokines into the intratumoral environment. Therefore, the combination of checkpoint blockade immunotherapy and PDT, can not only promote systematic immune response to inhibit the metastatic tumors, but also induce synergistic therapy for treating primary tumors. Recently, there are some reports describing porphyrinic MOFs for synergistic immunotherapy and PDT due to easy construction of porphyrinic MOFs-based nanoplatforms.
Zhang et al. reported benzoporphyrinic NMOFs (TBP-NMOF) for PDT, and further investigated synergistic PDT and α-PD-1 checkpoint blockade immunotherapy (Figure 8).[70] TBP-NMOF was assembled by the coordination of tetrabenzoporphyrin linkers with 10-connected $Z r_{6}$ clusters, which exhibited an efficient $^10_{2}$ generation ability for PDT under the hypoxic microenvironment. The results demonstrated that TBP-NMOF-induced PDT could not only kill the 4T1 murine breast tumor cells, but also aggrandize the presentation of tumor-activated antigens, further stimulate adaptive immune response for initiating the secretion of inflammatory cytokines $(1F N-y,$ TNF-α) and recruiting tumorinfiltrating T cells $\scriptstyle(\mathsf{C D4^{+}}$ , $\mathsf{C D8^{+}})$ . Further, they investigated the antitumor effect of low $O_{2}$ -dependent PDT and α-PD-1 in vivo. With the injection of PD-1 antibody, TBP-NMOF-induced PDT can dramatically inhibit the growth of 4T1 tumors without body weight loss. Furthermore, the combination therapy was more efficient than only PDT in preventing tumor recurrence and remarkably prevented lung metastasis of 4T1 tumor for 22 days. Similarly, Lin et al. also utilized porphyrinic MOFs (Fe-TBP) and $\mathtt{a}$ -PD-L1 for synergistic PDT and immunotherapy.[56a] It is interesting that the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}$ clusters in Fe-TBP NMOFs could overcome hypoxia by Fenton reaction and enhance immunogenic PDT for priming tumor immunotherapy.
Obviously, the use of porphyrinic MOFs for synergistic immuno- and photodynamic therapy has shown great potentials for therapy of many difficult-to-treat tumors and inhibition of metastatic tumor cells, because immunogenic PDT can prime tumor immunotherapy to promote the response rates, and thereby significantly promote immunotherapeutic efficacy. In addition to checkpoint blockade immunotherapy based on antibodies or small molecule agents, it can be imagined that the use of porphyrinic MOF platforms to combine PDT with other immunotherapeutic strategies would be attractive.[71]
