## 3.2.4. Other synergistic therapy
RT, that uses ionizing irradiation such as X-ray to destroy localized solid tumors, is a common therapy modality in clinic. Numerous nanoplatforms containing high-Z elements, which are able to interact with ionizing radiation to produce photo/auger electrons and then produce reactive free radicals to destroy tumor cells, have been applied for radiotherapy of tumors.[72] Biocompatible and biodegradable porphyrinic MOFs are formed by the coordination of porphyrin linkers and metal nodes, which is ideal to introduce high-Z metal elements into MOFs for the usage in synergistic PDT and RT. Liu et al. reported Hf-TCPP NMOFs assembled by $\mathsf{H}\mathsf{f}^{4+}$ and TCPP through a solvothermal process,[44] in which TCPP linkers exhibited good PDT ability, and $\mathsf{H}\mathsf{f}^{4+}$ metal center as a high-Z element was served as a radio-sensitizer to enhance RT. Due to the enhanced ISC by heavy $\mathsf{H}\mathsf{f}^{4+}$ nodes, HfTCPP NMOFs were extremely effective in producing $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ upon 661 nm irradiation for PDT. In vivo results on 4T1 breast tumorbearing mice showed that synergistic PDT and RT with Hf-TCPP NMOFs greatly inhibited tumor growth and had enhanced therapeutic efficacy compared to PDT or RT alone.
Nowadays, gas therapy also has been developed for tumor therapy due to its negligible side effect and enhanced therapeutic efficacy. Various gaseous transmitters, such as hydrogen sulfide $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S})$ , nitric oxide (NO) and carbon monoxide (CO), can be utilized for gas therapy that can accelerate tumor cell apoptosis, prevent tumor cell proliferation, and selectively protect the activity and physiological function of normal cells.[73] Recently, Cheng et al. reported synergistic NO gas therapy and PDT to treat tumors based on L-arginine (L-Arg)-incorporated PCN-224 NMOFs (Figure 9).[52c] Here, L-Arg, a natural NO donor, could release NO for gas therapy via oxidization of ROS, which provided by PDT process of PCN-224 upon 660 nm irradiation. In vitro MTT assay showed that the viability of 4T1 cells treated with L-Argincorporated PCN-224 was lower than that treated with PCN-224 upon $660~\mathsf{n m}$ irradiation. Of special note, cell apoptosis assay executed by flow cytometry showed L-Arg-incorporated PCN-224 had a considerable improvement of PDT under hypoxic condition compared to pure PCN-224, proving that NO can sensitize PDT in hypoxic tumors. Furthermore, in vivo antitumor study showed that the therapeutic efficacy of PCN-224 group was weaker than the L-Arg-incorporated PCN-224 group when irradiated under 660 nm light, indicating that synergistic PDT and gas therapy enhanced antitumor effects to a certain extent. Such nanoplatform with gas therapy and PDT provided a novel therapy paradigm to overcome the hypoxia barrier for efficient tumor treatment.
Hydrogen gas as clean energy has recently been discovered to have curative effects in many diseases, and it possesses huge potentials in tumor treatments due to its high biosafety and satisfactory therapeutic effect. He et al. developed a nanoscale Pd-MOF, which was assembled by $\mathsf{P d}^{2+}$ nodes and TPyP porphyrin linkers, for hydrogenothermal therapy of tumor.[74] Due to the strong hydrogen-binding and catalytic hydrogenation capacity of Pd, Pd-MOF could be used to deliver highly reductive hydrogen gas. The results showed that Pd-MOF possessed high hydrogen loading capacity and continuous hydrogen release profile. The photothermal experiments exhibited that Pd-MOF loaded with hydrogen gas possessed higher photothermal conversion efficiency $(44.2\%)$ than only Pd-MOF $(27.7\%)$ at 808 nm irradiation. Further in vitro and in vivo results proved the synergistic antitumor effects of hydrogen gas and PTT.
Interestingly, selectively cutting off the nutrient supply and metabolism pathways of tumor cells is another efficient strategy for tumor therapy. Zhang et al. designed a tumor targeted cascade bioreactor mem@catalase@GOx@PCN-224 (mCGP) by the encapsulation of glucose oxidase (GOx) and catalase in tumor cell membrane-camouflaged PCN-224 NMOFs for synergistic starvation and PDT.[52b] It was found that mCGP could facilitate microenvironmental oxygenation by catalyzing endogenous ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ to generate $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ by catalase, which would subsequently accelerate the consumption of intracellular glucose by GOx and increase the generation of $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ under 660 nm irradiation. It could cut off tumor glucose supply and disturb glucose metabolism related cellular elements for starvation therapy as well as overcome the hypoxia issue for enhanced PDT. In vivo experiments revealed that mCGP possessed a certain antitumor activity without $660\ \mathsf{n m}$ irradiation for its long-term starvation effects. On the other hand, mCGD showed a complete suppression of 4T1 tumor in the presence of 660 nm irradiation without obvious side effects, which might be attributed to the significant synergistic effects of PDT and long-term tumor starvation therapy.
## 3.3. Imaging-guided therapy of porphyrin-based MOFs
Nanomaterials have been comprehensively studied as novel imaging agents or functional platforms for bio-imaging, which produce signals or enhance signal contrast at specific tissues. Bio-imaging enables the visualization, evaluation and quantification of biological processes at the molecular and cellular levels in a noninvasive mean, and show great potentials in the earlier disease diagnosis, on-going assessment of treatment process and optimization of disease therapeutic protocols.[23b] Therefore, the construction of imaging-guided therapy nanosystems by the integration of therapy modalities and bioimaging techniques, are promising to maximize the therapeutic efficacy through time- and position-resolved manners.[75] Over the years, porphyrin-based MOFs have been utilized as promising fluorescence imaging-guided therapy nanosystems owing to their excellent fluorescent property and therapeutic ability. In addition, imaging modes of porphyrin-based MOFs can be branched to magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), X-ray computed tomography (CT), photoacoustic imaging (PAI) and photothermal imaging (PTI) by employing metal nodes in MOFs, metal centers in porphyrin rings or incorporating other contrast agents in MOFs.
## 3.3.1. Fluorescence imaging-guided therapy
Fluorescence imaging has been a powerful imaging mode for both in vitro and in vivo imaging with non-invasion and high signal sensitivity, which relies on the properties of fluorophores that absorb the photo energy in a certain band of wavelengths and then emit new photon energy in a band of longer wavelengths. Fluorescence imaging is rapid and suitable for high-throughput screening.[76] Porphyrins as popular fluorophores have been incorporated into MOFs for both in vitro and in vivo fluorescence imaging.[52a, 77] The use of fluorescence imaging to guide therapy allows the visualization of distribution and evolution of the tumor/particle during treatments, and thereby ensures selectively controllable therapy with high efficacy and safety. Actually, fluorescence imaging-guided therapy nanosystems based on porphyrin-based MOFs can be easily constructed for efficient therapy due to the combination of fluorescence and photosensitivity from porphyrins.
In 2017, Yin et al. developed for the first time biocompatible porphyrinic NMOFs for fluorescence imaging-guided tumor therapy (Figure 10).[37a] Porphyrinic NMOFs (NPMOFs) with a high TCPP content $(59.8\%)$ achieved efficient fluorescence imaging. The results showed that the fluorescence of TCPP was observed at $651~\mathsf{n m}$ with a weaker shoulder peak at 710 nm when excited at $514\ \mathsf{n m}$ , but only one emission peak at 689 nm for NPMOFs.[78] Strong red emission and long Stokes shift of NPMOFs induced a high signal-to-noise ratio and resolution in fluorescence imaging due to the low excitation interference, autofluorescence and scattering light from the biological tissue. In mice mode, the absorption, distribution, metabolism, and excretion (ADME) processes of NPMOFs were observed by tracking fluorescent trajectory, indicating the biocompatibility of NPMOFs in mammals. Furthermore, in HepG2 tumor-bearing mice mode, in vivo fluorescence of NPMOFs clearly confirmed the distributions of NPMOFs in tumors with high resolution and signalto-noise ratio, suggesting that porphyrinic NMOFs are promising as fluorescence imaging-guided therapy platforms for earlier tumor diagnosis and enhanced therapy. On the other hand, composite porphyrinic NMOFs have also been constructed for fluorescence imaging-guided therapy.[38a, 79] For example, Zhang et al. assembled porphyrinic NMOFs on AuNR to form core-shell nanocomposites for fluorescence imaging-guided PDT, PTT and chemotherapy.[38a] The introduction of porphyrinic NMOFs endowed the nanocomposites with the abilities of PDT, chemotherapy and fluorescence imaging, while AuNR exhibited excellent PTT ability.
In addition, fluorescence imaging-guided therapy can also real-time monitor and assess the therapeutic efficacy according to the changes of fluorescence signals, and further improve the therapeutic efficacy and avoid under- or over-treatment. Lei et al. designed a photosensitive and caspase-responsive porphyrin@MOF nanoprobe, which was assembled by porphyrin (TMPyP), folate targeting-motif and a caspase sensitive fluorescent dye, i.e., Cy3-labelled caspase-3 substrate peptide into MOFs.[45] Here, the fluorescence of Cy3 quenched until the activated caspase-3 to specifically cleave the peptide and the $\mathtt{C y3}$ released from MOFs for fluorescence imaging, resulting in a caspase-responsive sensing strategy for monitoring cell apoptosis. These results demonstrated that biocompatible TMPyP $@$ MOFs possessed enhanced $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ yield and folate targeting tumor therapy as well as the ability for in situ imaging of therapeutic efficacy via caspase-3 activation. The switch-on signal provides an effective way to image intracellular caspase activity for real-time monitoring and evaluation of treatment effects.
## 3.3.2. MRI-guided therapy
MRI is another noninvasive medical method to obtain high spatial resolution images of human anatomy and physiological processes. It utilizes an external magnetic field to detect radiofrequency signals produced from protons, typically of hydrogen atoms in water from fat and tissues. The acquired signals can be utilized to compare different tissues, distinguish lesions from healthy tissues, and build the anatomical maps of human body.[80] Compared to fluorescence imaging, MRI possesses the advantages of better spatial resolution, higher soft tissue contrast and unconfined penetration depth, but exhibits the disadvantage of low sensitivity. MRI contrast agents are typically used to reduce proton relaxation times and further improve the image quality. For porphyrin-based MOFs, the metal nodes of MOF skeleton, the chelation of paramagnetic metal ions in porphyrin rings or the incorporation of other contrast agents in MOFs make MRI-guided therapy possible.
Recently, Wang et al. reported that gadolinium porphyrinic NMOF sheets (Gd-TCPP) possessed high relaxation rate (40.8 $\mathsf{m}\mathsf{M}^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1})$ ) and $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ production upon $660~\mathsf{n m}$ irradiation due to high $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ content in MOFs and TCPP photosensitizers.[33d] Therefore, Gd-TCPP nanosheets are potent for ${\sf T}_{1}$ -weighted MRI-guided PDT. Yin et al. proposed to chelate paramagnetic metal ions into porphyrin rings to develop metalloporphyrinic MOFs for $T_{1^{-}}$ weighted MRI-guided therapy.[81] By chelating paramagnetic Mn ions into porphyrin rings, metalloporphyrinic MOFs possessed excellent T1-weighted MR contrast capacity and high photothermal conversion and heat-responsive NO release. In vivo experiments showed that metalloporphyrinic MOFs were efficiently accumulated at tumor site after intravenous injection into mice by MRI, and the tumor was completely inhibited when exposed to $808\mathsf{n m}$ laser for PTT and NO gas therapy, indicating the potential for MRI-guided therapy.
## 3.3.3. Multi-mode imaging-guided therapy
Single-mode bio-imaging is usually short of capabilities for comprehensive imaging and cannot provide entire characteristics for diagnosis of diseases due to its inherent limitations, such as limited signal sensitivity, tissue penetration depth and spatial resolution. To overcome these problems, various studies have concentrated on the construction of multi-mode imaging behaviors into a single MOF-based system.[82] For porphyrinic MOFs, the use of functional components of MOFs themselves or assembling other imaging agents can achieve multi-mode imaging-guided therapy systems, which could avoid inherent limitations of single imaging-guided therapy and provide more accurate positioning of lesions/particles as well as guidelines of therapy.
For example, Yin et al. developed a biocompatible nanocomposite $(\mathsf{F e_{3}O_{4}@C@P M O F})$ with fluorescence imaging and MRI functions for dual-mode imaging-guided phototherapy (Figure 11).[79] Specifically, $F e_{3}O_{4}@\mathsf C$ cores were selected as $\mathsf{T}_{2^{-}}$ weighted MRI contrast agents and photothermal agents, and porphyrinic MOF shells had fluorescence imaging and PDT abilities. Such dual-mode imaging nanoplatforms achieved high sensitivity of fluorescence imaging as well as deep penetration and great spatial resolution of MRI, and more precise in vivo information can be provided for enhancing the safety and therapeutic efficacy. After intravenous injection of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{C@P M O F}$ nanocomposites in MCF-7 tumor-bearing mice for 22 h, the fluorescence imaging showed that nanocomposites were primarily localized in the liver region than other organs. The tumor area was slowly brightened and became the brightest site in the mice after $26\mathrm{~h~}$ , indicating that the accumulation of nanocomposites at tumor site was gradually increased. The results were also confirmed by dramatic dimming at tumor site in ${\sf T}_{2}$ -weighted MR imaging. Motivated by their high tumor accumulation, $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{C@P M O F}$ nanocomposites were used for imaging-guided phototherapy, and exhibited obvious PTT/PDT synergetic effect for MCF-7 breast tumor but low toxicity for normal tissues.
Clearly, it is also valuable to develop porphyrinic MOFs without additional components for multi-mode imaging-guided therapy due to its simplicity and efficiency. Wu et al. reported ultrathin 2D Cu-TCPP MOF nanosheets for dual-mode imagingguided phototherapy.[67] The ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ nodes in $\mathtt{C u}$ -TCPP nanosheets offered excellent photothermal performance for PTT, PTI and ${\sf T}_{1}$ - weighted MRI. Meanwhile, porphyrin TCPP provided the ability to produce $^10_{2}$ for PDT. The results indicated that Cu-TCPP nanosheets had huge potentials for synergistic PTT and PDT, guided by PTI and MRI.
CT is an important medical imaging technique, which is attributed to attenuation of $\mathsf{X}$ -rays by a specimen to offer 3D images with high spatial resolution.[83] The high-Z number elements in MOFs can be chosen as CT contrast agents. In addition, PAI is a recently developed imaging technique that integrates optical excitation and ultrasound detection,[84] and shows the superiority of high selectivity and deep penetration, and can acquire high resolution and strong contrast tissue images. To combine the merits of PTI, CT and PAI, Yang et al. developed multifunctional metalloporphyrinic NMOF (Zr-FeP) shuttles as an “all-in-one” nanoplatform to realize tri-mode imaging-guided therapy.[66] In vivo PAI experiments demonstrated that much stronger PA signal was accepted in the tumor tissue after tail vein injection of nanoshuttles into mice for 2 h, indicating the considerable tumor accumulation of nanoshuttles. Zr-FeP MOF nanoshuttles exhibited good CT capability due to the high-Zr component for CT imaging. Additionally, the outstanding photothermal performance of Zr-FeP MOF nanoshuttles endowed remarkable PTI ability in vivo, and after treatment with Zr-FeP MOF nanoshuttles the tumor site temperature rapidly raised by $17^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ upon exposure to $635~\mathsf{n m}$ laser for 5 min. Therefore, the fascinating tri-mode imaging capability of Zr-FeP MOF nanoshuttles would be a powerful tool for precise tumor diagnosis and imaging-guided therapy.
