## 3.4. Biosensing
A large number of physiological species play a key role in regulating cellular functions and physiological activities, of which the changes in the concentrations and types indicate disruptions or pathological changes in physiological environments. Therefore, the specific and sensitive biosensing systems have been increasingly used to detect and monitor the variations for disease diagnosis and biological processes in living organisms. Porphyrins and their derivatives have been widely studied as small molecule biosensors, due to their excellent physicochemical characteristics that can be used for signal interaction with host molecules. Moreover, porphyrins can mimic many biological functions. For example, porphyrins can reversibly bind with gaseous compounds and can operate photophysical and/or redox processes mediated by the target analytes.[8a] Interestingly, hemin, which also called iron protoporphyrin IX, has been utilized for biosensing, because it can act not only as a redox mediator based on the electrochemical activity of Fe (III)/Fe (II) reversible redox, but also as a catalyst based on the peroxidase-like activity. In recent years, MOFs have also been extensively employed to construct biosensors due to their unique merits.[17b, 76a, 85] Especially, the porous structure and high specific surface area of MOFs provide high catalytic activity for target molecules. In addition, the designed shape and size as well as specific chemical interactions between guest molecules and MOFs endow them with high selectivity over target molecules. Incorporating porphyrins into MOFs can remit self-quenching of porphyrins and realize the collection of multiple functionalities of MOFs and porphyrins for biosensing with high sensitivity, resolution and precision. To date, porphyrin-based MOFs have been utilized for biosensing applications including electrochemical (EC), fluorescence, photoelectrochemical (PEC), electrochemiluminescence (ECL) and colorimetric biosensors. Various studies have demonstrated the possibility of the porphyrin-based MOFs-based biosensors for detecting different biological substances and states, mainly including biomacromolecules, small molecules, ions, pH values and others. Some typical examples of porphyrin-based MOFs for biosensing are summarized in Table 1-3.
## 3.4.1. Biosensing of biomacromolecules
DNA is the most vital genetic material of living beings, and various strategies have been proposed to detect DNA for diagnosis of diseases and biological processes. Among these strategies, EC biosensing has been extensively adopted for the detection of specific DNA owing to the high sensitivity and selectivity.[85] For DNA electrochemical biosensors, porphyrinbased MOFs with excellent electrocatalytic activity can be utilized as outer probes for the production and amplification of electrochemical signal.[26d, 86] For example, due to the peroxidaselike catalytic activity of TCPP(Fe) metalloporphyrins, Lei et al. encapsulated TCPP(Fe) into HKUST-1 and subsequently conjugated with streptavidin (SA) to construct an smart signaltransduction platform for hairpin DNA detection (Figure 12AC).[86a] Here, a label-free electrochemical DNA biosensor was designed by using TCPP(Fe)@MOF-SA as a signal probe and allosteric switch of hairpin DNA. The allosteric switch of hairpin DNA on the glassy carbon electrode (GCE) surface could form SA aptamer, that selectively binds the synthetic TCPP(Fe)@MOF-SA via biorecognition affinity. Thus, the conjugated TCPP(Fe)@MOF-SA on the GCE surface as mimetic catalyst could greatly enhance the oxidation of $o$ -phenylenediamine (oPD) to 2,2’-diaminoazobenzene in the presence of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ , as a fine electrochemical signal readout indicator. Such “signal-on” electrochemical biosensor has been proved to detect hairpin DNA with a detection limit down to 0.48 fM $(\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{N}=3)$ , 6-order magnitude linear range and good feasibility in complex serum matrixes.
On the other hand, porphyrinic MOFs for fluorescence biosensing has also been developed for DNA detection by utilizing the fluorescence quenching mechanism.[33c, 87] For example, Wang et al. used ultrathin $\mathtt{C u}$ -TCPP MOF nanosheets to design a fluorescence biosensor for rapid, sensitive, and multiplex detection of DNAs.[87] Due to the large surface area and large number of accessible active sites on the nanosheets, three dyelabeled ssDNA probes could be easily absorbed on the surface, leading to fluorescence quenching of the dye-labeled ssDNA by Förster resonance energy transfer (FRET). When specific target was hybridized with the ssDNA probes to form dsDNA, the formed dsDNA detached from Cu-TCPP MOF nanosheets owing to the weak physical adsorption between Cu-TCPP MOF nanosheets and dsDNA, and thereby the fluorescence “turn-on”. Consequently, Cu-TCPP MOF nanosheets presented a great ability to quantitatively detect pathogenic DNA with detection limit in pM level.
Immunoassay is of great interest for the clinical early diagnosis of various tumors, such as prostate specific antigen (PSA) analysis for diagnosis of prostate cancer. PEC bioanalysis is a potential bioanalytical method based on the photoinduced electron transfer (PET) processes among analyte, photoactive species and electrode with photoirradiation, which has been widely used in the detection of various biological molecules. Recently, Zhang et al. reported a new non-enzyme PEC immunoassay using DNA-mediated nanoscale PCN-222 to detect PSA.[88] The ss-DNA-tagged antibody (Ab-DNA) was conjugated on PCN-222 to form Ab-DNA-functionalized NMOFs complex (Ab-DNA-NMOFs). Ab-DNA-NMOFs, as an active photocathode PEC substrate, presented a remarkably enhanced photocurrent response with the presence of dopamine (DA) on $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ -containing aqueous media. Further functionalized with polyamidoamine dendrimer, Ab-DNA-NMOFs could serve as an efficient PEC signal transduction system for non-enzyme PSA immunoassay with hypersensitivity, with a broad calibration range of $1\ p\mathsf{g\ m L}^{-1}$ to $10\mathrm{ng}\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ and a detection limit of $0.2\mathsf{p g}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ .
In addition, ECL, an electrochemical redox-induced light emission, possesses several advantages including low background interference, simple operation, high sensitivity and space-time controllability. Han et al. proposed to use porphyrin@MOFs for PSA sensing via ECL strategy.[89] In this study, Fe-MIL-88 MOFs were used as a platform to load hemin and gold nanoparticles (Au NPs) and further graft $\mathsf{G}$ -quadruplex DNAzyme for synergetic catalytic amplification of luminol. The pDNA-Au-Hemin-MIL-DNAzyme probes were acted as quenchers and enhancers in fabricating a target-induced ratiometric ECL biosensor for PSA detection. The results demonstrated $\wedge$ that pDNA-Au-Hemin-MIL-DNAzyme-based biosensor exhibited highly sensitive and accurate analytical property for PSA with a linear detection range from 0.5 to 500 ng $\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ and a detection limit of 0.058 ng mL-1 $(\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{N}=3)$ .
Enzymes and proteins play important roles in the metabolic regulation, cell growth and other biological processes. Some of these biomolecules have been accepted as important biomarkers for early stage diseases diagnosis, prognosis judgement and pathogenesis study. Several different types of porphyrin-based MOFs have been used in the construction of electrochemical biosensor to enhance electrochemical signal for enzyme or protein biosensing. Yuan et al. reported multifunctional heminencapsulated Fe-MIL-88 MOFs, which were the first example in construction of an electrochemical aptasensor for thrombin (TB) detection (Figure 12D-F).[26c] Such porphyrin@MOFs were served as support for the immobilization of recognition molecules (thrombin binding aptamer (TBA)), as well as conjugated with GOx for signal amplification. GOx could oxidize glucose into gluconic acid accompanied by the production of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ , which could be further electrocatalyzed by hemin to amplify the electrochemical signal. Such electrochemical biosensor showed a relatively low detection limit of $0.068~\mathsf{p M}$ and a wide linear ranged from 0.0001 nM to 30 nM with a high correlation coefficient of 0.9961, indicating that the constructed biosensor could be used to detect TB quantitatively. Lei et al. designed an electrochemical biosensor that was composed of iron metalloporphyrinic NMOFs (PorMOF) for telomerase activity detection.[90] PorMOF exhibited an excellent electrocatalytic activity towards ${{\mathrm O}_{2}}$ reduction, which induced to detect telomerase with a detection limit of 30 cell mL-1 and assess the telomerase activity even in single Hela cell. Moreover, porphyrin-based MOFs were also applied to construct PEC and ECL biosensors for protein detection. For example, Zhang et al. developed a simple PCN-222 based PEC biosensor for label-free detection of a phosphoprotein (α-casein).[91] Shan et al. utilized zinc metalloporphyrinic MOFs (MOF-525(Zn)) to develop an ECL biosensor for highly sensitive protein kinase A (PKA) activity assay.[92]
## 3.4.2. Biosensing of small molecules
Various porphyrin-based MOF biosensors with peroxidaselike activity have been developed for detection of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ and glucose, which are important biological indicators in clinical medicine. For instance, Liu et al. synthesized MIL-101(Al)- $N H_{2}$ MOFs to anchor hemin for the formation of $\mathsf{H@{M}}$ composites, which showed peroxidase-like activity capable of catalyzing oxidation of the peroxidase substrate 3,3,5,5- tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) by $H_{2}O_{2}$ to produce the blue color in solutions.[93] Thus, $\mathsf{H@{M}}$ composites offered a simple, sensitive and selective colorimetric approach for ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ determination, and the results showed that $H_{2}O_{2}$ could be measured as low as $2\upmu\upmu$ and the linear range was from $5~{\upmu\upmu}$ to $200~{\upmu\upmu}$ with a correlation coefficient of 0.997. Furthermore, by introducing $\mathsf{G O x}$ as a
catalyst into $\mathsf{H@{M}}$ composites for oxidation of glucose, a MOFbased colorimetric biosensor was constructed for quantitative analysis of glucose in aqueous solution. The glucose biosensing was observed to have a linear range from $10~\upmu\upmu$ to $300\upmu\mathsf{M}$ with a correlation coefficient of 0.996.
Monitoring and quantifying some of gas levels (e.g., $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S})$ in living cells are of great importance in clinical medicine, because the gas concentrations are correlated with many human physiological and pathological processes. For example, Lin et al. developed novel phosphorescence/fluorescence dual-emissive NMOFs (R-UiO) for ratiometric intracellular $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ quantification (Figure 13),[36b] which was the first example of MOFs-based intracellular $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ biosensor. R-UiO NMOFs were synthesized by the coordination of Pt(II)-porphyrin linkers DBP(Pt) as an $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ -sensitive probe and amino-functionalized quaterphenyldicarboxylate linkers $(Q P D C-N H_{2})$ with Hf ions, and then covalently attached with Rhodamine B isothiocyanate (RBITC) as an $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ -insensitive reference probe via thiourea bonds. The DBP(Pt) and RBITC shared the same excitation energy, but the phosphorescence/fluorescence emissions did not interfere with each other, which allowed for the determination of intracellular ${{\mathrm O}_{2}}$ levels. When mouse colon carcinoma CT26 cells were treated with R-UiO NMOFs under different $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ pressure $(p0_{2})$ conditions at 4 mmHg, 32 mmHg and $160\:\mathrm{mmHg}$ , which represented hypoxia, normoxia and aerated conditions respectively, the analytic data by ratiometric luminescence images were $5.1\pm2.5$ mmHg, $27.3{\pm}3.1\$ mmHg and $158{\pm}11\ \mathrm{mmHg}$ , respectively. The results matched well with the preset $p{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ , suggesting the wide range and high accuracy of R-UiO NMOFs as intracellular oxygen biosensors. Tang et al. reported aluminum-copper mixed-metal porphyrinic MOFs (PAC) assembled by ${\mathsf{A l}}^{3+}$ nodes and TCPP(Cu)
linkers as heterogeneous fluorescence probe for the sensing of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}$ in living cells.[94] The $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ centers in MOF frameworks induced the fluorescence quenching of TCPP porphyrins, which could be used as ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}$ -responding site for fluorescence “turn-on” sensing of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}$ . Consequently, this biocompatible fluorescence probe exhibited high selectivity and sensibility as well as low toxicity in in situ sensing of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}$ under a physiological environment.
As a biochemical messenger, DA plays a critical role in neurotransmitters and neuromodulators in organisms that affects human activities. For measuring DA release from living cells, Yamauchi et al. designed an electrochemical biosensor using composite porphyrinic MOFs (MOF-525-PEDOT NTs),[95] which was made up of conductive poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene) nanotubes (PEDOT NTs) coated with MOF-525 nanocrystals. The synergistic conductivity and catalytic performance of MOF525-PEDOT NTs enhanced the detection ability for DA with a linear concentration range $(2~\upmu\mathsf{M}-270~\upmu\mathsf{M})$ and detection limit $(0.04\upmu\mathsf{M})$ .
Nowadays, rapid and sensitive detection of organophosphorus pesticides has gained more and more attentions due to the environmental pollution and human health risks. Su et al. synthesized Cu-H MOFs/NECF composites by ballflower-like Cu-hemin MOFs grown on the fibers of 3D nitrogencontaining melamine carbon foam (NECF) and the immobilization of AChE on Cu-H MOFs/NEC for trichlorfon detection.[96] The CuH MOFs/NECF-based biosensor exhibited great performance for trichlorfon detection with a board linear range $(0.25{\sim}20\ \mathsf{n g}\ \mathsf{m L}^{-1})$ , low detection limit (0.082 ng mL-1) as well as good toughness, and thereby facilitated trace detection of organophosphorus pesticides.
## 3.4.3. Ion sensing
Several metal ions are essential elements in human body and play vital roles in cellular activities, while their excesses would be harmful for health. Especially, heavy metal ions are easy to accumulated in human body via the food chain, and thereby result in adverse effects. Obviously, it is of great importance to selectively detect metal ions in cells or in environments. For porphyrin-based MOFs, the coordination capability of metal ions in porphyrin rings endows them with the ability to construct fluorescence “turn-off” or “turn-on” biosensors. In 2013, Li et al. proposed to use porphyrinic MOF-525 as a fluorescence “turn-off” biosensor for $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ sensing.[97] The porphyrin moieties in MOF-525 provided accessible recognition sites for fluorescence quenching response to trace amount of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ ions due to energy/charge transfer process. MOF-525 fluorescent probes showed great potentials for rapid and specific detection of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ in practical water samples and copper-related diseases likes Wilson and Alzheimer's diseases. Recently, a novel fluorescent porphyrinic MOF probe, where the UiO- $66(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ MOF was encapsulated into PCN-224, was developed by Lu et al. for detecting $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ in complex water environments.[98] Similarly, due to the fluorescence quenching ability of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ to the probe, this fluorescent probe exhibited excellent $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ detection ability with a LOD value as low as $0.068\mathsf{n M}$ , which is better than the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ concentration limitation in drinking water regulated by WHO.
Compared to fluorescence “turn-off” biosensors, fluorescence “turn-on” biosensors can avert the wrong response to sensing signals and more conveniently identify in a dark background.[36c] For example, Jiang et al. developed a fluorescence “turn-on” biosensor based on ${\mathsf{P d}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ metalloporphyrinic MOFs (PCN-222(Pd)) for $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ sensing.[99] Here, PCN-222(Pd) was used as catalytic Heck reaction system to produce highly fluorescent “turn-on” signal in the presence of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , due to a stronger binding affinity of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ over $\mathsf{P d}^{2+}$ to the nitrogen atoms in the porphyrin and the conversion of nonfluorescent aniline to fluorescent ring-closing product via ${\mathsf{P d}}^{0}.$ - catalyzed Heck-reaction. Consequently, PCN-222(Pd) biosensor exhibited highly selective and sensitive sensing of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ in aqueous environments with a very low detection limitation. In addition, porphyrinic MOFs were also utilized in construction of electrochemical biosensors for detecting metal ions.[100]
Additionally, although anions sensing is more challenging, selective sensing of anions by porphyrin-based MOFs has also been reported. For instance, Cao et al. developed $Z r$ -based MOFs with the mixed-linkers of 1,3,6,8-tetra (4-carboxylphenyl) pyrene (TBAPy) and TCPP $(Z\mathsf{r}(\mathsf{T B A P y})_{5}(\mathsf{T C P P}))$ , as a fluorescence “turnon” biosensor for $\mathsf{S}^{2-}$ sensing in aqueous environments.[36c] The results showed that the fluorescent intensity of $\mathsf{Z r}(\mathsf{T B A P y})_{5}(\mathsf{T C P P})$ was intensified remarkably after adding ${\mathsf{S}}^{2-}$ , but other anions including $\mathsf{S O}_{4}{}^{2-}$ , CNS−, ${\mathsf{C O O H}}^{-}$ , Br−, I−, ${\mathsf{I O}}_{3}^{-}$ , F−, ${\mathsf{H}}{\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{O}}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ and ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ did not alter its fluorescent intensity. Eddaoudi et al. encapsulated TMPyP(Pt) porphyrins into rhoZMOF for selectively sensing of various anions in aqueous and methanolic solutions.[8c]
## 3.4.4. pH sensing
Generally, pH plays an important role in maintaining the stability, normal morphology and function of cells and organisms. Due to the spectral characteristics changed by protonation/deprotonation of the pyrrole ring in porphyrins under varied pH,[101] porphyrin-based MOFs could be used to construct pH sensors, showing great potentials in pH monitoring, disease diagnosis and even evaluating therapeutic efficacy. In 2013, Zhou et al. reported for the first time Zr-based porphyrinic MOF PCN225 and metalloporphyrinic MOF PCN-225(Zn) for pH sensing.[35b] The results demonstrated that the fluorescence intensity of PCN-225 has a strong correlation with pH value of the solution when the pH in the range of $0\sim10.2$ , and the most acidic solution showed the weakest fluorescence while the pH 10.2 solution gave the highest intensity. Furthermore, $7{\sim}10$ was the most sensitive pH range for intensity response. Lu et al. developed an effective dual-emission fluorescent MOF composite probe (RB-PCN) that constructed by the encapsulation of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles into PCN-224 and following a modification with RBITC.[102] The results showed that RB-PCN could sensitively detect a board range of pH changes, and could also monitor the pH changes in living cells by fluorescence confocal imaging and determine the pH values in actual water samples by fluorescence spectra.
## 3.5. Other applications of porphyrin-based MOFs
In addition to the biomedical applications as mentioned above, porphyrin-based MOFs also have valuable applications in other biological related fields. For example, Guo et al. synthesized spherical Cu-TCPP(Cu) MOFs, and subsequently encapsulated Ag nanoparticles into MOFs to form Ag/Cu-TCPP(Cu) composites for antibacterial application.[103] The results showed that antibacterial effect of Ag/Cu-TCPP(Cu) composites was superior to that of penicillin, and the cytotoxicity of Ag/Cu-TCPP(Cu) composites was lower than that of naked Ag nanoparticles and Ag ions in vitro. Furthermore, Ag/Cu-TCPP(Cu) composites could also significantly promote the wound healing in vivo.
More recently, Qu et al. utilized ultrathin Cu-TCPP(Fe) MOF nanosheets to physically adsorb GOx for constructing a composite nanocatalyst for in vivo wound healing.[104] Here, CuTCPP(Fe)/GOx could continuously convert nontoxic glucose into gluconic acid and ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ by GOx, following by the pH decrease from 7 to $3{\sim}4$ that could dramatically activate the peroxidase-like activity of Cu-TCPP(Fe) MOF nanosheets. Subsequently, the produced ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ could be further transformed to the toxic •OH by the Fenton reactions of Cu and Fe ions. In vitro and in vivo experiments showed such cascade catalytic conversion from nontoxic glucose to toxic •OH enabled Cu-TCPP(Fe)/GOx nanocatalyst with robust antibacterial effect and negligible biotoxicity.
## 4. Toxicity and safety concerns
Regarding of the porphyrin-based MOFs for biomedical applications, the probable toxicity of porphyrin-based MOFs needs to be highly concerned. Considering the construction of MOFs from metal nodes and organic linkers, it is a priority to choose non-toxic or hypotoxic building components for improving the biocompatibility and reducing the toxicity. Fortunately, porphyrins as biocompatible motifs are beneficial for the improvement of MOFs’ biosafety. However, the toxicity of MOFs was found to be closely related to their metal nodes.[76a] The biocompatible metal ions (e.g., Zr, Fe, Zn, Cu) are possible to be nodes for constructing friendly porphyrin-based MOFs due to their acceptable toxicity for biomedical applications.[106] Especially for the synthesis of composite porphyrinic MOFs, the additional functional components also need to be considered for potential threats to the tissues and organismis. In addition to the composition of porphyrin-based MOFs, the physicochemical natures of MOFs including size distribution, shape, surface chemistry and dose-depending properties are also important factors in terms of toxicology.[106-107] All these parameters strongly affect the absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion, biodegradation and clearance of porphyrin-based MOFs, and further determine their potential toxicity in vivo. To date, these aspects of porphyrin-based MOFs have not been fully investigated while the multifunctionality have been deeply explored in most biomedical studies.
Understanding the intricate interactions between the physicochemical property, biological performance and biosafety could contribute to the successful translation to practical biomedical applications. Therefore, the relevant studies of porphyrin-based MOFs in biomedical applications need to be developed in depth. Firstly, it is important to fully use various progressive characterization techniques to accurately determine the physicochemical parameters of MOFs (e.g., size, shape, surface chemistry, porosity) and reasonably evaluate their merits and limitations, for understanding and ultimately modulating the structure-function relationship for MOFs.[108] Next, investigating the correlation between MOFs and biological systems is the base of correlating the MOFs’ features with the toxicity results. Recently, Faria et al. proposed the MIRIBEL (Minimum Information Reporting in Bio-Nano Experimental Literature) standard for different studies on bio-nano interactions, which normalizes material characterization, biological characterization and experimental details for enhancing reproducibility and quantitative comparisons of bio-nano materials.[109] In the development of porphyrin-based MOFs for biomedical applications, such standard should be referenced to facilitate safety evaluation and material optimizations. Furthermore, great progress has been made with respect to mechanism-based hazard assessment of nanomaterials and advanced tools have emerged for their safety evaluation.[110] Therefore, not only for porphyrin-based MOFs, researchers need to accelerate the use of emerging methods and tools, combining research results with bioinformatics, to scientifically and quantitatively correlate the data outcome of biomaterials and their toxicity results for their practical applications in biomedicine.
## 5. Conclusions and perspectives
Porphyrins and their derivatives have been often used as photosensitizers for PDT, fluorophores for fluorescence imaging and sensors for biosensing due to their excellent photophysical properties. As a new type of porous materials, MOFs possess high porosity, tunable structure, multifunctionality, biocompatibility and biodegradability. Studies have demonstrated that the construction of porphyrin-based MOFs can not only overcome the limitations of free porphyrins like instability and self-quenching issues in physiological environments, but also integrate the functionalities of MOFs and porphyrins as well as improve the physicochemical properties. In this review, recent achievements and progresses on the construction of porphyrin-based MOFs and their biomedical applications have been highlighted and discussed.
To date, various methods have been used to construct porphyrin@MOFs, porphyrinic MOFs and composite porphyrinic MOFs in order to meet the requirements of biomedical applications. Among them, many strategies including heavy atom substitution of porphyrinic MOFs, controlling the particle sizes, controllable design and modification with targeting moieties or functional components were proposed to enhance therapeutic efficacy of porphyrin-based MOFs in PDT. Additionally, rational design of functional ingredients in MOFs or adjuvant agents enable interfere survival mechanisms and alter tumor microenvironment to achieve better results in terms of PDT efficacy. Obviously, the optimized construction of porphyrinbased MOFs shows great potentials for enhanced PDT of tumors.
Another efficient strategy for enhancing therapeutic efficacy is the implementation of multi-modality therapy in a single system to overcome the limitations of single modality therapy. Porphyrinbased MOFs have exhibited a great capacity of combining PDT with other therapy modalities, including chemotherapy, PTT, immunotherapy and gas therapy, to achieve synergistic effects for maximizing therapeutic efficacy and improving safety. However, the majority of current studies focuses on dual-modality therapy, but there are few reports on tri- or more modality therapy based on porphyrin-based MOFs, which might offer more significant therapeutic effects.
Porphyrin-based MOFs have also been developed for imaging-guided therapy to further optimize the therapeutic process and thereby enhance therapeutic efficacy. Due to excellent fluorescent property and photosensitization of porphyrins, porphyrin-based MOFs could realize fluorescence imaging-guided therapy. On the other hand, metal nodes in MOFs or the incorporation of contract agents in MOFs endow porphyrinbased MOFs with the ability of MRI-, CT-, PAI- or PTI-guided therapy. Such “all in one” nanoplatforms provide more accurate diagnosis and guidance for treatment, and are of great attractive for tumor therapy.
Besides the exploration of tumor therapy functions, porphyrin-based MOFs have been utilized to construct biosensors for detecting various biological ingredients, such as biomacromolecules, small molecules, ions, pH values and gases. Owing to the combination of excellent physicochemical natures of MOFs and porphyrins, porphyrin-based MOFs types of biosensors show significant performance in biosensing with high sensitivity and selectivity, acceptable reproducibility, good stability and biocompatibility, and some porphyrins including hemin, TCPP and its metal derivatives are often used to construct porphyrin-based MOFs type of biosensors. However, it can be anticipated that the biosensing performance could further improved by seeking more interesting porphyrins. On the other hand, porphyrin-based MOF type of biosensors for cell sensing and in vivo sensing need to further investigate for the development of biosensors in living biological systems.
Despite the growing number of impressive progress reports on porphyrin-based MOFs, their development in biomedical applications is still at the early stage facing many challenges. For porphyrin@MOFs, the porphyrin loading capacity, premature leakage and self-quenching of porphyrins should be considered during the synthesis process. Actually, porphyrinic MOFs are more promising candidates for biomedical applications. However, the influence of size, shape, composition and surface chemistry of porphyrin-based MOFs on therapeutic efficacy and biosensing ability should be further optimized to meet the requirements of personalized therapy, diagnose and detection. On the other hand, design of “all in one” nanoplatforms based on composite porphyrinic MOFs would be an efficient strategy to enhance therapeutic efficacy in tumor therapy and biosensing ability. Actually, researchers should establish the straightforward synthesis protocols for those designed MOF materials, as the simpler the preparation, the more likely it is that the product will be relevant for biomedical applications in the real-world. Finally, it needs to re-emphasize that thoroughly and deeply investaging the correlation between the fundamental parameters of porphyrinbased MOFs and their biosafety including the pharmacokinetics, biodistribution, biocompatibility, long-term toxicity and clearance from the body, are crucial for possible clinical translations. In conclusion, porphyrin-based MOFs have presented great potentials for the applications in tumor therapy and biosensing, and more advancements and progresses in biomedical field will be achieved in the future.
